WEBVTT

00:00:00.300 --> 00:00:03.035
In this video, we'll learn moving frames,

00:00:03.035 --> 00:00:06.638
coordinates that travel along
with a train or a storm,

00:00:06.638 --> 00:00:10.173
and see how they're used
to find a storm's own winds,

00:00:10.173 --> 00:00:12.775
and what a barometer sees as it passes.

00:00:13.575 --> 00:00:18.375
Imagine riding along with a storm
as it crosses the English Channel.

00:00:18.725 --> 00:00:23.800
Would its winds look the same to you as
they do to someone standing on the shore?

00:00:24.150 --> 00:00:27.575
This is the fifth of seven
parts on coordinates.

00:00:27.925 --> 00:00:31.335
Now let's dive into moving
frames: what changes,

00:00:31.335 --> 00:00:35.100
and what stays the same, when
the frame itself moves.

00:00:35.450 --> 00:00:38.018
Then we'll ride along with a real storm,

00:00:38.018 --> 00:00:41.550
and see what a barometer
on the coast saw as it passed.

00:00:42.350 --> 00:00:45.500
Let's start with the simplest
moving frame.

00:00:45.850 --> 00:00:49.237
Imagine you're on a train
gliding along a straight

00:00:49.237 --> 00:00:52.150
track at a steady twenty
metres per second.

00:00:52.500 --> 00:00:56.450
You walk towards the front at one
and a half metres per second.

00:00:56.800 --> 00:01:00.201
Someone standing beside
the track sees your speed and

00:01:00.201 --> 00:01:04.950
the train's speed added together:
twenty one and a half metres per second.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:08.425
Why do the speeds add?

00:01:08.775 --> 00:01:11.675
Let's measure positions along the track.

00:01:12.025 --> 00:01:16.575
The person beside the track uses x,
measured from the station.

00:01:16.925 --> 00:01:20.700
You use x prime, measured
from the back of the train.

00:01:21.050 --> 00:01:24.756
The back of the train passed
the station at time zero,

00:01:24.756 --> 00:01:28.325
and it moves at capital U, twenty
metres per second.

00:01:28.675 --> 00:01:34.400
So after a time t, the back of the train
is capital U times t from the station.

00:01:35.900 --> 00:01:38.606
Your distance from the station is that,

00:01:38.606 --> 00:01:44.225
plus your distance from the back of the
train: x equals x prime plus capital U t.

00:01:44.575 --> 00:01:48.825
And both clocks show the same
time: t prime equals t.

00:01:49.625 --> 00:01:53.384
Next come two short derivations:
why speeds add,

00:01:53.384 --> 00:01:56.125
and why accelerations don't change.

00:01:56.475 --> 00:01:59.050
They're for those who like every step.

00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:02.082
If not, skip ahead to the time on screen:

00:02:02.082 --> 00:02:06.400
you don't need them for the intuition
of weather, climate or maps.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.775
Now let's get the speeds
from the positions.

00:02:10.125 --> 00:02:15.836
In a short time, delta t, your place
on the train changes by delta x prime,

00:02:15.836 --> 00:02:20.100
and the back of the train
moves on by capital U delta t.

00:02:20.450 --> 00:02:24.891
So the change seen from the station
is the sum of the two:

00:02:24.891 --> 00:02:28.950
delta x equals
delta x prime, plus capital U delta t.

00:02:30.450 --> 00:02:33.125
Next, we divide by delta t.

00:02:33.475 --> 00:02:38.725
Delta x over delta t is the speed
seen from the ground, small u.

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:44.675
Delta x prime over delta t is the speed
seen on the train, u prime.

00:02:45.025 --> 00:02:48.200
So u equals u prime plus capital U.

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:54.925
With our numbers, your walking speed plus
the train's speed gives twenty one and

00:02:54.925 --> 00:02:56.950
a half metres per second again.

00:02:58.450 --> 00:03:01.238
Switching between two frames like this,

00:03:01.238 --> 00:03:04.383
moving at a steady
velocity without turning,

00:03:04.383 --> 00:03:08.600
is called a Galilean transformation,
after Galileo Galilei.

00:03:08.950 --> 00:03:12.958
In three dimensions,
the same holds for each part:

00:03:12.958 --> 00:03:16.725
bold v equals bold
capital U plus bold v prime.

00:03:17.525 --> 00:03:19.675
Here's the beautiful part.

00:03:20.025 --> 00:03:22.525
What happens to accelerations?

00:03:22.875 --> 00:03:26.500
Let's follow the same
steps, one move at a time.

00:03:26.850 --> 00:03:31.750
First, at every moment, u equals
capital U plus u prime.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:38.100
Next, a short time later, each of them has
changed a little, and the sum still holds.

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:42.706
So delta u equals delta
capital U plus delta u prime:

00:03:42.706 --> 00:03:46.400
the change of a sum
is the sum of the changes.

00:03:47.900 --> 00:03:50.450
Then we divide by delta t.

00:03:50.800 --> 00:03:54.925
Now let delta t shrink, as
in the derivatives movie.

00:03:55.275 --> 00:04:01.925
That gives d u by d t equals d capital
U by d t, plus d u prime by d t.

00:04:03.425 --> 00:04:09.325
Finally, the train's speed never changes,
so d capital U by d t is zero.

00:04:09.675 --> 00:04:13.209
So d u by d t equals d u prime by d t:

00:04:13.209 --> 00:04:17.300
the acceleration
is the same in both frames.

00:04:18.800 --> 00:04:21.299
For example, if you speed up from one and

00:04:21.299 --> 00:04:24.104
a half to two metres
per second in one second,

00:04:24.104 --> 00:04:27.701
the person beside the track
sees you go from twenty one and

00:04:27.701 --> 00:04:30.200
a half to twenty two, in the same second.

00:04:31.700 --> 00:04:33.715
Drop a ball on a smooth train,

00:04:33.715 --> 00:04:38.953
and it speeds up downwards by nine point
eight metres per second every second,

00:04:38.953 --> 00:04:41.975
for you, and for the person
beside the track.

00:04:42.325 --> 00:04:46.889
And since Newton's law says force
equals mass times acceleration,

00:04:46.889 --> 00:04:50.400
the same forces explain
the motion in both frames.

00:04:50.750 --> 00:04:56.255
If you skipped, here's what to keep: in a
frame moving steadily in a straight line,

00:04:56.255 --> 00:04:58.841
speeds change by the frame's own speed,

00:04:58.841 --> 00:05:02.025
but accelerations,
and so forces, stay the same.

00:05:02.825 --> 00:05:05.536
In a fluid, we follow an air parcel:

00:05:05.536 --> 00:05:09.075
a small volume of air
that moves with the wind.

00:05:09.425 --> 00:05:11.082
In the derivatives movie,

00:05:11.082 --> 00:05:15.125
we found the rate that an air
parcel's own thermometer reads.

00:05:15.475 --> 00:05:21.409
Capital D T by D t equals partial
capital T by partial small t,

00:05:21.409 --> 00:05:23.575
plus bold v dot grad T.

00:05:23.925 --> 00:05:27.450
The first part is the change
at one fixed place.

00:05:27.800 --> 00:05:29.376
The second is advection:

00:05:29.376 --> 00:05:34.300
the change from being carried to places
where the temperature is different.

00:05:34.650 --> 00:05:39.935
The eastward wind, u, is just another
number the parcel carries along,

00:05:39.935 --> 00:05:42.200
so we can put u in place of T.

00:05:42.550 --> 00:05:49.375
Capital D u by D t equals partial u by
partial t, plus bold v dot grad u.

00:05:49.725 --> 00:05:52.750
The same goes for v and for w.

00:05:54.250 --> 00:05:59.350
So the air parcel's acceleration
is a local change at a fixed place,

00:05:59.350 --> 00:06:00.475
plus advection.

00:06:00.825 --> 00:06:04.450
Now here's the train's lesson,
written for a fluid.

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:10.150
On the left is the parcel's acceleration,
worked out by someone on the ground.

00:06:10.500 --> 00:06:13.768
On the right is the same
parcel's acceleration,

00:06:13.768 --> 00:06:18.217
worked out by someone riding
in a frame that moves at capital U,

00:06:18.217 --> 00:06:20.025
with primes on everything.

00:06:20.375 --> 00:06:25.175
They're equal, because the acceleration
is the same in both frames.

00:06:25.525 --> 00:06:28.375
But each piece on its own
can be different.

00:06:28.725 --> 00:06:30.825
Let's see that with numbers.

00:06:31.625 --> 00:06:34.810
Picture a pattern
of stronger and weaker winds,

00:06:34.810 --> 00:06:39.350
two hundred kilometres from one
gust to the next, all blowing east.

00:06:39.700 --> 00:06:43.937
The whole pattern slides east
at a steady ten metres per second,

00:06:43.937 --> 00:06:45.725
without changing its shape.

00:06:46.075 --> 00:06:47.115
Take one moment,

00:06:47.115 --> 00:06:51.793
at a place where the wind is thirteen
point five four metres per second,

00:06:51.793 --> 00:06:55.301
and is getting stronger
towards the east by zero point

00:06:55.301 --> 00:06:58.550
one one one metres per second
for every kilometre.

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:05.125
First, ride with the pattern,
at capital U, ten metres per second.

00:07:05.475 --> 00:07:10.025
To you, the pattern is frozen, so
the local change is zero.

00:07:10.375 --> 00:07:11.438
Relative to you,

00:07:11.438 --> 00:07:16.686
the air at that place moves east at the
wind's speed minus the pattern's speed:

00:07:16.686 --> 00:07:22.200
three point five four metres per second,
or twelve point seven kilometres per hour.

00:07:22.550 --> 00:07:24.764
It's carried into stronger wind,

00:07:24.764 --> 00:07:28.845
so the advection is that
speed times the slope of the wind,

00:07:28.845 --> 00:07:32.650
zero point one one one metres
per second per kilometre.

00:07:33.000 --> 00:07:37.300
That's plus one point four
metres per second, every hour.

00:07:38.800 --> 00:07:44.625
Now stand on the ground, beside an
anemometer, a wind meter that never moves.

00:07:44.975 --> 00:07:48.951
The pattern slides past at thirty
six kilometres per hour,

00:07:48.951 --> 00:07:51.625
bringing the weaker wind from the west.

00:07:51.975 --> 00:07:57.908
So the anemometer sees a local change of
minus the pattern's speed times the slope:

00:07:57.908 --> 00:08:01.625
minus four point zero metres
per second, every hour.

00:08:03.125 --> 00:08:05.805
The advection now uses the full wind,

00:08:05.805 --> 00:08:08.992
forty eight point seven
kilometres per hour,

00:08:08.992 --> 00:08:12.975
times the same slope, which
gives plus five point four.

00:08:14.475 --> 00:08:16.342
Adding the local change and

00:08:16.342 --> 00:08:20.975
the advection gives plus
one point four, the same as for the rider.

00:08:22.475 --> 00:08:25.377
So each piece depends on who measures it,

00:08:25.377 --> 00:08:29.200
but their sum, the air
parcel's acceleration, doesn't.

00:08:29.550 --> 00:08:33.625
That only works for a frame moving
steadily in a straight line.

00:08:33.975 --> 00:08:38.595
A frame that turns, like the spinning
Earth, adds the Coriolis and

00:08:38.595 --> 00:08:42.725
centrifugal effects, as we saw
in the cross product movies.

00:08:43.075 --> 00:08:47.750
We'll build all of this fully in a later
movie: The material derivative.

00:08:48.550 --> 00:08:50.750
Now a real moving storm.

00:08:51.100 --> 00:08:54.007
Storm Ciarán crossed
the English Channel in the

00:08:54.007 --> 00:08:56.913
night from the first
to the second of November,

00:08:56.913 --> 00:08:58.150
twenty twenty three.

00:08:58.500 --> 00:09:01.404
To follow it, we find its lowest pressure,

00:09:01.404 --> 00:09:07.075
then look for the lowest pressure close
by an hour later, and so on, hour by hour.

00:09:07.425 --> 00:09:12.643
At midnight, universal time, its centre
was just off the tip of Cornwall,

00:09:12.643 --> 00:09:16.575
at nine hundred and fifty
four and a half hectopascals.

00:09:18.075 --> 00:09:20.749
Between midnight and six in the morning,

00:09:20.749 --> 00:09:24.627
the centre moved towards a bearing
of seventy one degrees,

00:09:24.627 --> 00:09:29.975
east-north-east, at about fourteen point
six metres per second, roughly fifteen.

00:09:31.475 --> 00:09:35.911
From Part 1, the maths angle
is ninety degrees minus the bearing,

00:09:35.911 --> 00:09:37.480
and an arrow's east and

00:09:37.480 --> 00:09:42.325
north parts are its length times the
cosine and the sine of that angle.

00:09:42.675 --> 00:09:47.934
So the storm's east part is its speed
times the sine of seventy one degrees,

00:09:47.934 --> 00:09:51.325
and its north part
is its speed times the cosine.

00:09:51.675 --> 00:09:55.563
Sine seventy one degrees is about
zero point nine four six,

00:09:55.563 --> 00:09:59.649
and cosine seventy one degrees
about zero point three two six,

00:09:59.649 --> 00:10:03.932
so the east part is about
thirteen point eight metres per second,

00:10:03.932 --> 00:10:06.700
and the north part about four point eight.

00:10:08.200 --> 00:10:11.514
We'll call this storm
velocity bold capital U:

00:10:11.514 --> 00:10:13.891
thirteen point eight times i hat,

00:10:13.891 --> 00:10:16.269
the local east arrow from Part 4,

00:10:16.269 --> 00:10:20.375
plus four point eight
times j hat, the local north arrow.

00:10:21.175 --> 00:10:25.709
Let's look at the winds on a ring two
hundred kilometres from the centre,

00:10:25.709 --> 00:10:27.200
at three in the morning.

00:10:27.550 --> 00:10:32.127
As in Part 1, we split each wind
into its part round the ring and

00:10:32.127 --> 00:10:36.000
its part in or out, with the
formulas we derived there.

00:10:36.350 --> 00:10:37.744
Seen from the ground,

00:10:37.744 --> 00:10:40.731
the round part averaged
about eighteen metres

00:10:40.731 --> 00:10:43.387
per second on the storm's southern side,

00:10:43.387 --> 00:10:46.175
but only about seven on its northern side.

00:10:47.675 --> 00:10:49.725
Why so lopsided?

00:10:50.075 --> 00:10:55.600
The storm spins anticlockwise as seen
from above, as northern storms do.

00:10:55.950 --> 00:10:59.669
On its southern side, the spin
blows towards the east,

00:10:59.669 --> 00:11:02.975
the same way the storm
moves, so the two add up.

00:11:03.325 --> 00:11:07.234
On its northern side, the spin
blows towards the west,

00:11:07.234 --> 00:11:10.275
against the motion, so they partly cancel.

00:11:11.075 --> 00:11:13.726
Now let's ask what the wind looks like to

00:11:13.726 --> 00:11:16.636
someone riding along
with the storm's centre,

00:11:16.636 --> 00:11:17.800
at bold capital U.

00:11:18.150 --> 00:11:20.778
That's called the storm-relative wind,

00:11:20.778 --> 00:11:23.752
and we met it briefly
in the vectors movie,

00:11:23.752 --> 00:11:26.450
as the wind minus the storm's velocity.

00:11:26.800 --> 00:11:28.612
From the Galilean rule,

00:11:28.612 --> 00:11:34.125
the ground wind is bold capital U plus
the rider's wind, bold v prime.

00:11:34.475 --> 00:11:39.175
So the rider's wind is the ground
wind minus bold capital U.

00:11:40.675 --> 00:11:43.544
Taking an arrow away is the same as adding

00:11:43.544 --> 00:11:46.550
that arrow turned
to point the opposite way.

00:11:46.900 --> 00:11:50.175
Take a point in the middle
of the southern side.

00:11:50.525 --> 00:11:55.725
There, the ground wind is thirteen point
five east and eight point four north.

00:11:56.075 --> 00:11:59.253
Take away the storm's
velocity, part by part,

00:11:59.253 --> 00:12:02.856
and the rider feels
minus zero point three east and

00:12:02.856 --> 00:12:07.800
three point seven north: a gentle
three point seven metres per second.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:12.675
Now a point in the middle
of the northern side.

00:12:13.025 --> 00:12:18.575
The ground wind is minus four point two
east and minus five point three north.

00:12:18.925 --> 00:12:21.322
Take away the same storm velocity,

00:12:21.322 --> 00:12:25.130
and the rider feels
minus eighteen point zero east and

00:12:25.130 --> 00:12:29.925
minus ten point one north: about
twenty point six metres per second.

00:12:31.425 --> 00:12:35.400
So for the rider, the northern
side is the windy one.

00:12:36.200 --> 00:12:40.575
Let's do the same with the round
parts, averaged over each side.

00:12:40.925 --> 00:12:45.703
On the southern side, the storm's
motion runs the same way as the spin,

00:12:45.703 --> 00:12:49.875
so it adds about thirteen metres
per second to the round part.

00:12:50.225 --> 00:12:55.325
Riding with the storm, the round part
there drops from eighteen to about five.

00:12:56.825 --> 00:13:00.666
On the northern side, the motion
runs against the spin,

00:13:00.666 --> 00:13:02.900
so it takes away about thirteen.

00:13:03.250 --> 00:13:08.500
Riding with the storm, the round part
there grows from seven to about twenty.

00:13:10.000 --> 00:13:13.316
So the motion explains
why people on the ground

00:13:13.316 --> 00:13:16.350
felt the southern side
as the stronger one.

00:13:16.700 --> 00:13:20.382
The rider sees the storm's
own shape, and at that hour,

00:13:20.382 --> 00:13:24.600
on this ring, its own winds were
stronger on its northern side.

00:13:24.950 --> 00:13:29.646
Forecasters use storm-relative winds
to see a storm's own structure,

00:13:29.646 --> 00:13:34.550
and they matter for thunderstorms
too, in a later movie: Severe storms.

00:13:35.350 --> 00:13:38.075
So what changes, and what doesn't?

00:13:38.425 --> 00:13:42.500
The wind at each point changes
by the storm's velocity.

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:47.152
But the ring average of the round
part, which we built in Part 1,

00:13:47.152 --> 00:13:50.527
is about fourteen metres
per second in both frames:

00:13:50.527 --> 00:13:55.425
fourteen point one from the ground, and
thirteen point eight riding along.

00:13:56.925 --> 00:14:02.025
And the average inflow is about six
metres per second in both frames.

00:14:03.525 --> 00:14:05.525
That's no accident.

00:14:05.875 --> 00:14:09.618
A steady, uniform wind blows
as much round one side of

00:14:09.618 --> 00:14:12.584
a circle as it blows back round the other,

00:14:12.584 --> 00:14:17.175
so it adds nothing to the average
spin, or to the average inflow.

00:14:17.525 --> 00:14:20.850
On a flat map, it adds exactly zero.

00:14:21.200 --> 00:14:25.123
On the round Earth, the meridians
close in towards the pole,

00:14:25.123 --> 00:14:28.850
and that leaves the small
difference of zero point three.

00:14:30.350 --> 00:14:35.525
The spin belongs to the storm, and the
motion belongs to the whole pattern.

00:14:35.875 --> 00:14:40.025
For a house on the coast, though, the
ground wind is what counts.

00:14:40.375 --> 00:14:45.448
That night, the weather station at Brest
measured a gust of eighty four knots,

00:14:45.448 --> 00:14:48.700
about a hundred and fifty
six kilometres per hour.

00:14:49.500 --> 00:14:54.000
The moving frame also explains
what a fixed barometer sees.

00:14:54.350 --> 00:14:57.575
Let's take Plymouth,
on England's south coast.

00:14:57.925 --> 00:15:00.898
Picture the pressure
pattern as a frozen map,

00:15:00.898 --> 00:15:03.475
sliding east-north-east with the storm.

00:15:03.825 --> 00:15:08.897
A barometer that never moves sees
the pressure fall as the low comes closer,

00:15:08.897 --> 00:15:12.100
even if the storm itself
isn't deepening at all.

00:15:12.450 --> 00:15:15.447
So the barometer's
reading mixes two things:

00:15:15.447 --> 00:15:18.375
the storm arriving,
and the storm changing.

00:15:18.725 --> 00:15:22.025
To pull them apart, we need two observers.

00:15:22.375 --> 00:15:25.225
The first is the fixed barometer.

00:15:25.575 --> 00:15:28.353
Its rate is partial p by partial t:

00:15:28.353 --> 00:15:32.400
the change of pressure
in time, at one fixed place.

00:15:32.750 --> 00:15:36.100
Here p is the pressure, and t is the time.

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.825
The second is a rider, moving with the
pressure pattern at bold capital U.

00:15:43.175 --> 00:15:47.198
The rider's position,
measured in the moving pattern,

00:15:47.198 --> 00:15:51.600
is x prime, which is x minus
capital U t, as on the train.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:55.875
And the rider's clock reads
tau, the Greek letter t.

00:15:56.225 --> 00:15:59.865
Tau is the same time as t, but
we give it a new name,

00:15:59.865 --> 00:16:03.025
because it marks a different
thing held fixed.

00:16:03.375 --> 00:16:07.872
Partial p by partial tau is the change
of pressure in time while

00:16:07.872 --> 00:16:11.175
x prime is held fixed:
riding with the pattern.

00:16:12.675 --> 00:16:17.400
If the pattern really were frozen, the
rider would see no change at all.

00:16:17.750 --> 00:16:21.934
So partial p by partial tau
is the storm itself changing:

00:16:21.934 --> 00:16:25.825
negative when it deepens,
and positive when it fills.

00:16:26.625 --> 00:16:28.775
How are the two rates linked?

00:16:29.125 --> 00:16:31.325
The next three steps show it.

00:16:31.675 --> 00:16:34.306
They're for anyone who
wants every step and

00:16:34.306 --> 00:16:36.937
aren't needed for the
intuition of weather,

00:16:36.937 --> 00:16:37.916
climate or maps,

00:16:37.916 --> 00:16:42.750
so skip ahead to the time on screen if
you'd rather go straight to the numbers.

00:16:43.100 --> 00:16:47.725
First, the rider is a traveller,
moving at bold capital U.

00:16:48.075 --> 00:16:52.195
In the derivatives movie, we
found the rate a traveller feels:

00:16:52.195 --> 00:16:54.055
the change at a fixed place,

00:16:54.055 --> 00:16:58.175
plus the traveller's speed times
the slope, in each direction.

00:16:58.525 --> 00:17:04.620
With the dot product, that's partial p by
partial t, plus bold capital U dot grad p,

00:17:04.620 --> 00:17:07.812
where grad p, as in the cross
product movie,

00:17:07.812 --> 00:17:11.150
points the way the pressure
increases fastest.

00:17:12.650 --> 00:17:17.675
Next, for this traveller,
that rate is partial p by partial tau.

00:17:18.025 --> 00:17:23.173
So partial p by partial tau
equals partial p by partial t,

00:17:23.173 --> 00:17:25.925
plus bold capital U dot grad p.

00:17:27.425 --> 00:17:31.825
Then we move bold capital U dot
grad p to the other side.

00:17:32.175 --> 00:17:37.059
Partial p by partial t equals
partial p by partial tau,

00:17:37.059 --> 00:17:39.900
minus bold capital U dot grad p.

00:17:41.400 --> 00:17:43.575
Now let's read each term.

00:17:43.925 --> 00:17:46.925
On the left is the barometer's rate.

00:17:47.275 --> 00:17:50.750
The first term on the right
is the storm changing.

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:54.881
And bold capital U dot
grad p is the storm's speed,

00:17:54.881 --> 00:17:59.625
times how fast the pressure rises
along its direction of motion.

00:17:59.975 --> 00:18:04.239
Kilometres per hour, times
hectopascals per kilometre,

00:18:04.239 --> 00:18:06.450
gives hectopascals per hour.

00:18:06.800 --> 00:18:11.925
With its minus sign, it's the change
caused by the pattern sliding past.

00:18:13.425 --> 00:18:15.943
If you skipped, here's what to keep:

00:18:15.943 --> 00:18:21.400
what the barometer sees is the storm
changing, minus the pattern sliding past.

00:18:22.200 --> 00:18:25.350
Now the numbers at Plymouth,
around midnight.

00:18:25.700 --> 00:18:29.556
At eleven in the evening,
the pressure was nine hundred and

00:18:29.556 --> 00:18:31.777
sixty four point one hectopascals,

00:18:31.777 --> 00:18:36.025
and at one in the morning,
nine hundred and fifty six point five.

00:18:36.375 --> 00:18:41.846
So, at midnight, the barometer's rate is
the later reading minus the earlier one,

00:18:41.846 --> 00:18:44.413
divided by the two hours between them:

00:18:44.413 --> 00:18:47.925
about minus three point eight
hectopascals per hour.

00:18:49.425 --> 00:18:52.550
Plymouth was ahead
of the low, about a hundred and

00:18:52.550 --> 00:18:55.175
twenty three kilometres to its north-east.

00:18:55.525 --> 00:18:59.368
There, the pressure rose
along the storm's direction by

00:18:59.368 --> 00:19:03.350
about zero point zero six one
hectopascals per kilometre.

00:19:04.850 --> 00:19:08.575
The storm moved at fourteen
point six metres per second,

00:19:08.575 --> 00:19:11.900
which is fifty two and a half
kilometres per hour.

00:19:12.250 --> 00:19:17.456
So bold capital U dot grad p is
the storm's speed times that slope:

00:19:17.456 --> 00:19:20.875
about three point two
hectopascals per hour.

00:19:22.375 --> 00:19:26.790
Then the storm's own change,
partial p by partial tau,

00:19:26.790 --> 00:19:31.450
is partial p by partial t plus bold
capital U dot grad p.

00:19:31.800 --> 00:19:33.990
Adding the barometer's rate and

00:19:33.990 --> 00:19:39.075
the sliding term gives about minus
zero point six hectopascals per hour.

00:19:40.575 --> 00:19:41.611
So, at Plymouth,

00:19:41.611 --> 00:19:45.364
the barometer's fall
of three point eight hectopascals per

00:19:45.364 --> 00:19:48.989
hour splits into zero point six
from the storm changing,

00:19:48.989 --> 00:19:52.225
and three point two
from the pattern sliding past.

00:19:53.725 --> 00:19:57.675
Most of what the barometer
saw was the storm arriving.

00:19:58.025 --> 00:20:01.025
Is the minus zero point six believable?

00:20:01.375 --> 00:20:03.025
Here's a check.

00:20:03.375 --> 00:20:04.931
Between eleven and one,

00:20:04.931 --> 00:20:10.071
the lowest pressure at the storm's
tracked centre fell from nine hundred and

00:20:10.071 --> 00:20:15.280
fifty four point nine to nine hundred
and fifty four point zero hectopascals:

00:20:15.280 --> 00:20:19.000
about minus half a hectopascal
per hour, the same size.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:25.250
This is the same idea as advection
in the dot product movie.

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:28.914
Forecasters draw maps
of these pressure changes,

00:20:28.914 --> 00:20:33.125
and we'll read them in a later
movie: Reading maps over time.

00:20:33.925 --> 00:20:34.705
To sum up,

00:20:34.705 --> 00:20:41.019
a frame moving steadily in a straight line
shifts positions by capital U times t,

00:20:41.019 --> 00:20:45.775
and velocities by capital U:
u equals u prime plus capital U.

00:20:46.125 --> 00:20:48.902
But it leaves accelerations unchanged,

00:20:48.902 --> 00:20:52.775
so the same forces explain
the motion in both frames.

00:20:53.125 --> 00:20:58.350
For an air parcel, the acceleration
is the local change plus advection.

00:20:58.700 --> 00:21:02.750
Each piece depends on who measures
it, but their sum doesn't.

00:21:03.100 --> 00:21:08.225
Storm Ciarán moved east-north-east
at about fifteen metres per second.

00:21:08.575 --> 00:21:13.667
Taking that motion away from every
wind gives the storm-relative wind:

00:21:13.667 --> 00:21:16.650
what a rider moving with the storm feels.

00:21:17.000 --> 00:21:19.383
It changed the wind at every point,

00:21:19.383 --> 00:21:24.150
but left the storm's average spin
of about fourteen metres per second.

00:21:24.500 --> 00:21:30.275
A fixed barometer's rate is the storm
changing, minus the pattern sliding past.

00:21:30.625 --> 00:21:31.474
At Plymouth,

00:21:31.474 --> 00:21:37.275
the fall of three point eight hectopascals
per hour was mostly the storm arriving.

00:21:37.625 --> 00:21:42.715
In Part 6, we'll find the shortest way
between two places on the round Earth:

00:21:42.715 --> 00:21:47.475
the great circle, and the rhumb line
that sailors steered for centuries.

00:21:47.825 --> 00:21:53.725
The great circle explains why flights from
Europe to California pass over Greenland.

00:21:54.525 --> 00:21:58.683
This video is one of a series
of related maths tutorials,

00:21:58.683 --> 00:22:00.725
so watch out for the others.

00:22:01.075 --> 00:22:05.472
They're made for students
of atmospheric science, meteorology and

00:22:05.472 --> 00:22:09.125
fluid dynamics, and anyone
else who needs these ideas.

00:22:09.475 --> 00:22:14.303
The notebook for coordinates, and the
notebooks for the other maths concepts,

00:22:14.303 --> 00:22:17.125
are on the website that
goes with this video.
